{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-43803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+1\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-4})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{6}+3\\cdot (\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(2+2i)+(-4)+12+(6-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =16-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43803" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-43803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12-2i"] ["math" "16+5i"] ["math" "16-5i"] ["math" "16-i"] ["math" "18+i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43803" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "16-i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-141039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2+i \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-141039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\overset{―}{(2+i)}+2\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(1+2i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{1}+1\\cdot \\overset{―}{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (2-i)+2\\cdot (-3)+(1+2i)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(-2+i)+(-6)+(1+2\\text{i})+2 \\\\  & =-5+3i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-141039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-141039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-141039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-5+3i"] ["math" "-5+i"] ["math" "2+3i"] ["math" "2+i"] ["math" "4+2i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-141039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-5+3i"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 1-6\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}+4 \\\\ \\text{i}+9 \\\\ -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1-4i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{i+4})+(1-6i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{i+9})+\\text{i}\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1-4i)\\cdot (4-i)+(1-6i)\\cdot (9-i)+\\text{i}\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =(-17i)+(3-55i)+(-1) \\\\  & =2-72i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-96829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12i"] ["math" "2-72i"] ["math" "20+56i"] ["math" "4-55i"] ["math" "5-27i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-72i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2-i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{3}+(2-i)(\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (1-i)+2\\cdot 3+(2-i)(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+6+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =11-4i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-43804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10-2i"] ["math" "11-4i"] ["math" "12-4i"] ["math" "14+i"] ["math" "2i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11-4i"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ -1 \\\\ -i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+(1+2i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1})+(-i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (1-i)+(1+2i)\\cdot (-1)+(-i)\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =(1+i)+(-1-2i)+1 \\\\  & =1-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-43808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1+2i"] ["math" "-1-2i"] ["math" "1+i"] ["math" "1-2i"] ["math" "1-i"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-43808" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1-i"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨a,b⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $i⟨a,b⟩=⟨a,-ib⟩$ for any  $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- There is vector $a\\in {C}^{n}$ such that  $⟨a,a⟩<0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. A complex number is equal to its conjugate if and only if it is a real number. The inner product over $C$  is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,-ib⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false.  The inner product over $C$ is positive-definite. So, for any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩\\ge 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,-ia⟩=-i⟨a,a⟩$ for any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, by the antilinearity in the second argument we have\n\n$$\n⟨a,-ia⟩=\\overset{―}{(-i)}⟨a,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\ne -i⟨a,a⟩\n$$\n\n \nfor any nonzero vector  $a\\in {C}^{n}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $a=0$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-96833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-96833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$  $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97258" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩$ if  \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=-1-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,(4-\\text{i})b+c⟩ & =⟨a,(4-\\text{i})b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c\\text{ }⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1-\\text{i}})+5 \\\\  & =(4+\\text{i})\\cdot (-1+\\text{i})+5 \\\\  & =-5+3\\text{i}+5 \\\\  & =3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97193" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "-5+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\text{i}"] ["math" "3\\text{i}"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97193" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,6b+c⟩$ if   \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=2+3\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=3+8\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,6b+c⟩ & =⟨a,6b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{6}⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+⟨a,c⟩ & =6(\\overset{―}{2+3\\text{i}})+(3+8\\text{i}) \\\\  & =6(2-3\\text{i})+(3+8\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-18\\text{i}+3+8\\text{i} \\\\  & =15-10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1+8\\text{i}"] ["math" "-5+7\\text{i}"] ["math" "15-10\\text{i}"] ["math" "3-5\\text{i}"] ["math" "7-\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15-10\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨a,\\,b+(2+5\\text{i})c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨b,a⟩=3-\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,c⟩=\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is antilinear in the second argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,\\,b+(2+5\\text{i})c⟩ & =⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,(2+5\\text{i})c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩+(\\overset{―}{2+5\\text{i}})⟨a,c⟩ \\\\  & =⟨a,b⟩+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨a,b⟩+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨b,a⟩$ and $⟨a,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}+(2-5\\text{i})⟨a,c⟩ & =\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}+(2-5\\text{i})\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =3+\\text{i}+(2-5\\text{i})\\cdot \\text{i} \\\\  & =3+\\text{i}+2\\text{i}-5(-1) \\\\  & =8+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "-5-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "8+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "8\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8+3\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=2+4\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨c,b⟩=-5-3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6a+4b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨6a,c⟩+⟨4b,c⟩ \\\\  & =6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6⟨a,c⟩+4⟨b,c⟩ & =6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨c,b⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}+4\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =6(\\overset{―}{2+4\\text{i}})+4\\overset{―}{(-5-3\\text{i})} \\\\  & =6(2-4\\text{i})+4(-5+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =12-24\\text{i}-20+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =-8-12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3+\\text{i}"] ["math" "-8-12\\text{i}"] ["math" "12+8\\text{i}"] ["math" "3-\\text{i}"] ["math" "8+12\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8-12\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $C,$ find $⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩$ if \n\n$\\,$ $⟨c,a⟩=1-2\\text{i}\\,$ and $\\,⟨b,c⟩=2+3\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4a-b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨4a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & =4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4⟨a,c⟩-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $⟨c,a⟩$ and $⟨b,c⟩:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{⟨c,a⟩}-⟨b,c⟩ & =4\\overset{―}{(1-2\\text{i})}-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4(1+2\\text{i})-(2+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4+8\\text{i}-2-3\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b9aea95bc2b0f3d0948763302c4c26ff7579af434895f23f3da082a34876c014"} {"question" "q-97209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\text{i}"] ["math" "2+5\\text{i}"] ["math" "5+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "5-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "7\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "q-97209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2+5\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/state.json" "file_sha256" "1336b66a713b8e91da0fcc68315f262ba48b9581572ed6790bda6b7f01365e34"} {"question" "e-5545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3,$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}i \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/example-5545.json" "file_sha256" "5c5ca25249e6c9fe32781904691d507b1f6ca9741d8d6e547bfda1df9ad64c20"} {"question" "e-5545" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(-2)\\cdot \\overset{―}{4+i}+0\\cdot \\overset{―}{5} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (4-i)+0 \\\\  & =-3i-8+2i \\\\  & =-8-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/example-5545.json" "file_sha256" "5c5ca25249e6c9fe32781904691d507b1f6ca9741d8d6e547bfda1df9ad64c20"} {"question" "e-5547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\langle \\mathbf{a},  -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n\n- $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/example-5547.json" "file_sha256" "9059555f6631fd204ad69db0b7821f4611c640c5fb712ea9af9acc0baf4a692f"} {"question" "e-5547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle  \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle =  \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle  = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\, } \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},- \\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any  $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument  and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n\n$$\n\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{ \\mathrm{i} }\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n \nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n.$ But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/example-5547.json" "file_sha256" "9059555f6631fd204ad69db0b7821f4611c640c5fb712ea9af9acc0baf4a692f"} {"question" "e-8578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C},$ find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/example-8578.json" "file_sha256" "22cd5b2ed375b44867beda82e6e5b77837d486fe2b268fa1022424dc9c479a69"} {"question" "e-8578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties. \n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C},$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n,$ we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨-2a+3b,\\,c⟩ & =⟨-2a,c⟩+⟨3b,c⟩ \\\\  & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3⟨b,c⟩ & =-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2⟨a,c⟩+3\\overset{―}{⟨c,b⟩} & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3\\overset{―}{(2-4\\text{i})} \\\\  & =-2(3+5\\text{i})+3(2+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =-6-10\\text{i}+6+12\\text{i} \\\\  & =2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040565/example-8578.json" "file_sha256" "22cd5b2ed375b44867beda82e6e5b77837d486fe2b268fa1022424dc9c479a69"}] "usage" {"q-43803" [] "e-5545" [] "q-96833" [] "e-8578" [] "q-43804" [] "e-5547" []} "basis" 1446 "retractions" [[17592186134349 :question/problem "Given that $x, y ∈ C^{4}$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x, y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186134350 :question/problem "Given that $x, y ∈ C^{3}$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x, y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 - \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186134471 :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ over $C$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨a, b⟩ = ⟨b, a⟩^(―)$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$\n2. $⟨a, αb⟩ = α⟨a, b⟩$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$ and any $α ∈ C$\n3. $⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$"] [17592186133495 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb C^3$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186133495 :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= x_{1}y_{1}^(―) + x_{2}y_{2}^(―) + x_{3}y_{3}^(―) \\\\\n&= \\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3^(―)) + (-2) \\cdot 4 + \\textrm{i}^(―) + 0 \\cdot 5^(―) \\\\\n&= \\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3) + (-2) \\cdot (4 - \\textrm{i}) + 0 \\\\\n&=-3\\textrm{i} - 8 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&=-8 - \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133496 :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ over $\\mathbb{C}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle\\in\\mathbb R$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n2. $\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, -\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$ for any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$\n3. $\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number for any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$"] [17592186133496 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}$.\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is a complex number in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n$\\langle \\mathrm{i}\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\mathrm{i}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\overline{\\, \\mathrm{i}\\,} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a},-\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$\nfor any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, by the linearity in the first argument and the antilinearity in the second argument, we have\n$\\langle\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a},\\mathrm{i} \\mathbf{a} \\rangle =\\mathrm{i}\\cdot \\bar{\\mathrm{i}}\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = -\\mathrm{i}^2\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle= \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$\nfor any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$. But by positive-definiteness, this is a nonnegative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186133604 :question/problem "Given that $\\langle \\,\\cdot\\,, \\,\\cdot\\, \\rangle$ is the standard Hermitian inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$, find $\\big\\langle -2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}, \\: \\mathbf{c} \\big\\rangle$ if\n\n$\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = 3+5\\text{i}, \\quad \\quad \\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = 2-4\\text{i}$."] [17592186133604 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ satisfies the following properties.\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\alpha \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\alpha \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{c} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle + \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle \\quad$ and $\\quad \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\alpha\\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha} \\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$.\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle}$.\n- It is positive-definite. For any $\\mathbf{a}\\in \\mathbb{C}^n$, we have$\\qquad$ $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle$ is a non-negative real number, and $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0$ if and only if $\\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n\nFirst, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ is linear in the first argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨ - 2a + 3b, c⟩ &= ⟨ - 2a, c⟩ + ⟨3b, c⟩ \\\\\n&=-2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨b, c⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that the inner product over $\\mathbb{C}$ has conjugate symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨b, c⟩ &= -2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨c, b⟩^(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{c} \\rangle$ and $\\langle \\mathbf{c}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2⟨a, c⟩ + 3⟨c, b⟩^(―) &= -2(3 + 5\\textrm{i}) + 3(2 - 4\\textrm{i})^(―) \\\\\n&=-2(3 + 5\\textrm{i}) + 3(2 + 4\\textrm{i}) \\\\\n&=-6 - 10\\textrm{i} + 6 + 12\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186132537 17592186132538 17592186132539 17592186132540 17592186132541 17592186132543 17592186132544 17592186132545 17592186132546 17592186132547 17592186133269 17592186133270 17592186133271 17592186133272 17592186133273] "immutable_fields" [[17592186132536 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132536 :answer-field/id] [17592186132536 :answer-field/key] [17592186132536 :answer-field/type] [17592186132542 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132542 :answer-field/id] [17592186132542 :answer-field/key] [17592186132542 :answer-field/type] [17592186133268 :answer-field/correct] [17592186133268 :answer-field/id] [17592186133268 :answer-field/key] [17592186133268 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
